OneiOS take18, userland/metal/patches/NativeMetal/0004: command buffers scheduled behind at most 3 uncompleted ones; the
render server's reads of other processes' surfaces wait for their writers.
Upstream: Droid-VM/NativeMetal 0e7550a (wip/hackintosh, HuJK, 2026-11-08, MIT), ported onto b0d6a07 without its
nmb_split_row hunk (that row comes with e4015b3/3a2d2ca, not taken): its scheduled-held and capped counts are on the
telemetry's "J scheduled wait" row instead.
What: (1) schedpace: a command buffer's scheduled handlers and waitUntilScheduled wait until fewer than 3 command buffers
committed before it on its queue are uncompleted (each completion lets the next through in commit order; none waits over
1 s; not in backboardd). (2) surfacesync::awaitWriters: backboardd, before it submits, asks the kernel to wait (at most
100 ms) for other processes' GPU work begun before it that writes a surface it reads: the host's KGSL rings do not order one
context after another, and the render server's ring runs ahead of the apps'. The kernel's virtio-gpu client
(DroidVMVirtioGpu3D.cpp: surfaceAccess pad 2 answers "ReadWait") has it; a kernel without it answers a ticket, which this
ends.
Why: WebKit paces a WebGPU canvas only on scheduling; the runtime scheduled every command buffer at submission, so a page
whose frames take longer than a refresh queued frame after frame (webgpu-samples deferredRendering on the board: 14.5-15
fps shown, 0.5 before). The reads' wait is what the kernel's test switch oneios-gpu-read-wait=1 forced (docs/gpu.md): a
composition that sampled a widget's or a canvas's surface before its drawing ran.
Switch: boot arguments oneios-sched-pace=0 (ONEIOS_SCHED_PACE=0: scheduled at submission, as before) and oneios-read-wait=0
(ONEIOS_READ_WAIT=0: the render server does not ask the kernel). Also upstream's file A/B: /private/var/ngpu/debug/
no-scheduled-pacing, scheduled-depth. Removable: yes (ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=scheduled-pacing-read-wait).

